Unveils the Next Generation of AI Robotics\n\n![   Generation 7 of ‘Phoenix’ robots include improved human-like range of 
motion      Improvements in uptime, visual perception, and tactile sensing increase the capability of the system to perform
complex tasks over longer periods    Design iteration significantly decreases build time     The speed at which new tasks 
can be automated has increased 50x, marking a major inflection point in task automation speed   
](data:image/svg+xml;base64,PHN2ZyB3aWR0aD0iMSIgaGVpZ2h0PSIxIiB4bWxucz0iaHR0cDovL3d3dy53My5vcmcvMjAwMC9zdmciPjwvc3ZnPg==)\n
\nGeneration 7 of ‘Phoenix’ robots include improved human-like range of motion Improvements in uptime, visual perception, 
and tactile sensing increase the capability of the system to perform complex tasks over longer periods Design iteration 
significantly decreases build time  The speed at which new tasks can be automated has increased 50x, marking a major 
inflection point in task automation speed\n\n**VANCOUVER, CANADA –** [Sanctuary AI](https://sanctuary.ai/), a company on a 
mission to create the world’s first human-like intelligence in general purpose robots, today unveiled the seventh 
generation of its general purpose robot Phoenix™. The latest generation robot and its AI control system, Carbon™, draw even
closer to that of a person, with wide ranging improvements to both the hardware and AI software. The announcement comes 
less than 12 months after Sanctuary AI’s sixth generation robot ([one of TIME’s best inventions of 
2023](https://sanctuary.ai/news/sanctuary-ai-named-on-time-best-inventions-of-2023-list/)) was launched, and less than 16 
months after its fifth generation robot was [first commercially 
deployed](https://sanctuary.ai/news/sanctuary-ai-deploys-first-humanoid-general-purpose-robot-commercially/) in January 
2023.\n\nThis generation of Phoenix is one of the most sophisticated human behavioral data capture technologies available 
today, providing Carbon with some of the highest quality, highest fidelity, training data available. The changes 
include:\n\n* Increased uptime, making robots more available for training and data capture\n* Increased build and 
commissioning speed, allowing us to bring more units online faster\n* Reduced bill of materials, lowering the cost of 
manufacture\n* Significant hardware improvements, increasing range of motion in the wrists, hands, and elbows, and 
increasing hand durability\n* Further miniaturized hydraulics, reducing the weight, power consumption, and complexity with 
increased hardware and software measures that exceed specified safety standards\n* Improved visual acuity and tactile 
sensing, leading to higher-fidelity data to train the AI control system\n* The time it takes for new tasks to be automated 
has gone from weeks to less than 24 hours, marking a major inflection point in task automation speed and autonomous system 
capability\n\n“It’s incredible to see the progress that has been made in just 11 months,” said Geordie Rose, former CEO and
Co-Founder of Sanctuary AI. “With Generation 7, we have a system that we believe is the most closely analogous to a person 
of any available. We see this as not only the cornerstone of general purpose AI robotics but a critical step on the path to
artificial general intelligence, and we’re thrilled to be leading the charge on it. This all adds up to being able to 
capture increasing quantities of higher quality, higher fidelity human behavioral data, which in turn will expedite the 
development of foundational AI models and customer 
deployments.”\n\n![](data:image/svg+xml;base64,PHN2ZyB3aWR0aD0iMSIgaGVpZ2h0PSIxIiB4bWxucz0iaHR0cDovL3d3dy53My5vcmcvMjAwMC9…
